"== Colour Realism #01 =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: Two wet cherries hang from one stem, rendered so realistically that the deep-red skin appears translucent around the highlights. Tiny condensation droplets magnify the cherry surface beneath them while reflected window light creates cool blue-white highlights. Delicious, glossy, irresistible. In the right panel it is on a woman, inner forearm, giving the small still life enough clean space for the droplets and reflections to read. Create exceptional contemporary Colour Realism tattoo artwork whose primary goal is convincing visual reality rendered through believable full color. Do not create an ordinary realism tattoo and simply add bright colors afterward. Build form through color, value, temperature, light, shadow, texture, reflection and atmosphere. REALISM MAKES IT BELIEVABLE. COLOR MAKES IT ALIVE. LIGHT MAKES THE COLOR MAKE SENSE. Begin every artwork with a strong value structure. Establish the primary light source, shadow structure, focal hierarchy and environmental light before determining saturation. Color must describe the physical world. Warm light should influence nearby surfaces. Cool environments should produce believable cool reflected color. Red fabric may cast subtle red into nearby skin. Green foliage may influence adjacent surfaces. Blue water should affect submerged objects and shadowed undersides. Neon must illuminate nearby skin and materials. Glass must transmit, distort and refract surrounding colors. Chrome must reflect its environment. Wet surfaces should carry believable reflections. Translucent petals, fruit, skin, water and other materials should transmit light appropriately. Use realistic color temperature throughout shadows rather than filling shadows with black. Preserve strong dark, middle and light values so the tattoo remains dimensional and readable. Vibrant does not mean uncontrolled. Saturation should support focal hierarchy. Some artwork may be intensely colorful. Some should use natural restrained color. Some may be predominantly warm or cool. Some may use muted environments with one extraordinary focal color. Suppress unnecessary hard outlines. Form should primarily emerge through changes in value, temperature, edge, texture and depth rather than graphic borders. Use sharp detail selectively at focal points and softer edges in supporting areas. Avoid making every square centimeter equally detailed. Render materials according to their real physical behavior. Skin must look like skin. Fur must follow growth direction and catch light naturally. Feathers must display believable structure and iridescence. Glass must refract. Water must distort and reflect. Metal must respond to the surrounding environment. Flowers and fruit should show translucency, moisture and surface variation where appropriate. Fabric should respond to weave, sheen and folds. The subject can be anything capable of believable rendering: portraits, human figures, animals, birds, insects, flowers, fruit, ocean, landscapes, objects, glass, chrome, fabric, architecture, cinematic scenes, surreal concepts and impossible worlds. Surreal imagery is fully allowed, but impossible subjects must still obey convincing light, material, perspective and atmospheric logic. Do not reduce Colour Realism to endless lions, tigers, womens faces, roses and eyes. Use those subjects when the artwork is exceptional, but explore the full world. Design specifically for the stated body placement. Do not paste rectangular photographs onto skin. Crop compositions naturally. Let subjects enter and leave the body area without artificial frames. Use shoulder curvature to enhance dimensional subjects. Let forearms and calves support strong vertical compositions. Give portraits, animals and complex scenes sufficient room. Use thighs for more detailed compositions and backs, chests and sleeves for the rare larger narrative work. Anatomy should influence movement, crop and visual hierarchy. Do not make every tattoo enormous. Most concepts should remain attainable small and medium pieces while retaining enough physical size for realistic color transitions and detail. Use fewer large pieces and only rare major body compositions. Avoid artificial HDR, plastic-looking skin, excessive saturation, neon colors without a believable light source, generic blurred backgrounds, unnecessary black outlines, flat color fill, muddy midtones, random color splashes, photographic rectangles, unrelated collage elements and impossible reflections that ignore environmental logic. IF THE TATTOO WOULD BE JUST AS POWERFUL IN BLACK AND GREY, ASK WHETHER COLOR IS ACTUALLY CONTRIBUTING SOMETHING IMPORTANT. COLOR SHOULD REVEAL SOMETHING BLACK AND GREY COULD NOT. REALISM MAKES IT BELIEVABLE. COLOR MAKES IT ALIVE. LIGHT MAKES THE COLOR MAKE SENSE. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  " + $u; $out += ("Colour Realism: " + $u) } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
"== Sketch #01 =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A huge raven is caught at the instant its wings open. The head and chest resolve through confident black drawing while the wings contain repeated searching contours, abandoned feather positions and long gestural marks that overshoot the final anatomy. Wild, intelligent, raw. In the right panel it is on a man, outer upper arm and shoulder, allowing the wing movement to spread around the deltoid. Create exceptional contemporary Sketch tattoo artwork where the visible drawing process is the defining visual language. THE DRAWING PROCESS IS THE STYLE. THE DRAWING LOOKS UNFINISHED. THE ARTWORK IS COMPLETELY INTENTIONAL. Do not create a polished tattoo and surround it with random scratches. Do not create ordinary Fine Line artwork with meaningless loose marks. Every unfinished mark must have a visual reason to exist. Use searching contours, repeated attempts, gesture lines, anatomical construction, centerlines, perspective guides, circles, ellipses, proportion marks, abandoned positions, overshooting strokes, hatching, cross-hatching, scribble shading, charcoal-like darkness, broken contours and selective areas of confident finished drawing. SOME LINES SEARCH. SOME LINES MEASURE. SOME LINES MOVE. SOME LINES COMMIT. Allow the viewer to understand how the artist discovered the final image. Use hierarchy. Not every mark should have equal strength. Preliminary construction can be lighter. Corrections can become more assertive. Final committed areas can use darker confident black. Allow some areas to remain almost unresolved while focal areas become sharply understandable. Movement is a major opportunity. Use repeated positions, ghosted limbs, abandoned wing locations, gestural arcs and overlapping anatomy to show where a body, animal or object has just been and where it is moving. Do not use generic cartoon speed lines. Sketch is a visual language, not a subject category. The subject can be anything. Portraits. Human figures. Hands. Animals. Birds. Horses. Dragons. Skulls. Skeletons. Demons. Angels. Dark fantasy. Gothic architecture. Mythological creatures. Horror. Botanicals. Machines. Motorcycles. Cars. Fashion. Architecture. Anatomy. Surrealism. Impossible creatures. Abstract drawing. Do not keep the imagery polite. Some tattoos should be beautiful. Some should be intimate. Some should be elegant. Some should be dark. Some should be aggressive. Some should be disturbing. Some should feel enormous and mythological. Some should make the viewer wonder what the hell they are looking at before the subject suddenly resolves. Push artistic ambition rather than novelty. Do not turn dark artwork into cheap gore or juvenile shock imagery. Darkness should come through composition, anatomy, mythology, mortality, psychological tension, scale, atmosphere and drawing energy. Black and natural skin should dominate much of the collection. Color may be used as part of the drawing process. Pale blue can represent preliminary construction. Red or burgundy can represent correction, alternative positioning or an additional drawing layer. Black can establish the final committed drawing. These relationships are possibilities rather than mandatory rules. Color must not become random decoration. Do not turn Sketch into Watercolour. Do not use full realistic color unless the concept specifically requires a hybrid. Hatching and cross-hatching should describe form and directional shadow. Scribble and charcoal-like marks should carry weight, movement or atmosphere rather than merely making the tattoo look dirty. Construction lines should relate logically to the subject. Human heads may use cranial masses, centerlines and eye axes. Hands may use joint circles and finger direction. Animals may expose skeletal or muscular construction. Machines and architecture may retain perspective axes and ellipses. Dragons and invented creatures should be constructed convincingly enough that their imaginary anatomy feels studied. Design every composition specifically for the stated body placement. THE BODY IS THE PAGE, BUT THE PAGE IS NOT FLAT. Use forearms for elongated drawings, perspective, smaller portraits and technical studies. Use upper arms and shoulders for portraits, creatures and compositions that benefit from wrapping construction. Use thighs for large figures, dark fantasy, horses, dragons and complicated drawing studies. Use calves for strong vertical compositions. Use ribs for gesture and elongated human figures. Use backs for rare major pieces where wings, anatomy, architecture or large drawing systems need space. Let construction and movement respond to body curvature rather than appearing printed onto the skin. Do not make every design huge. Most should remain wearable small and medium pieces. Use large scale where the artistic idea genuinely benefits from it. Reserve full backs, chest compositions and major limb projects for exceptional concepts. Avoid repetitive wolves, lions, roses and womens faces unless the treatment is genuinely exceptional. Avoid random sketch marks. Avoid fake handwritten notes. Avoid meaningless arrows. Avoid decorative geometry unrelated to construction. Avoid generic splatter. Avoid clean finished tattoos with superficial scribbles around them. Avoid making every drawing equally chaotic. Contrast beautifully resolved areas against unresolved areas. THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A BAD SKETCH AND A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO IS INTENTION. A BAD SKETCH LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE IT WAS NOT FINISHED. A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE THAT IS THE FINISHED ART. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  " + $u; $out += ("Sketch: " + $u) } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
"== Skulls & Skeletons #01 =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: An anatomically observed human skull is shown from an unusual three-quarter underside angle so the jaw, teeth, cheekbone and base of the cranium become the composition. Fine Black and Grey rendering treats the skull like a beautiful anatomical study rather than a death emblem. Anatomical, intelligent, severe. In the right panel it is on a man, outer forearm. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo inspiration centered on skulls, skeletons and bone. BONE IS THE MATERIAL. DEATH IS NOT THE ONLY SUBJECT. Skulls and skeletons can express: Anatomy. Death. Life. Movement. Love. Loneliness. Strength. Fear. Beauty. Time. Nature. Science. History. Transformation. Darkness. Faith. Architecture. And pure fascination with skeletal form. UNDERSTAND THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A SKULL AND A SKELETON. A skull is primarily a form. Study it. Rotate it. Light it. Fracture it. Abstract it. Open it. Reconstruct it. Transform it. Use unusual anatomical viewpoints. Do not default to a centered front-facing skull. A skeleton is a body. USE BODY LANGUAGE. Skeletons can: Walk. Run. Dance. Fight. Pray. Reach. Fall. Climb. Embrace. Protect. Carry. Swim. Ride. Rest. Lie down. Curl inward. Stretch upward. Skeletons do not need facial expression to communicate emotion. Posture is the expression. ANATOMY MATTERS. When anatomy is presented as realistic or scientific, respect actual skeletal structure. Understand: Skull. Jaw. Teeth. Spine. Vertebrae. Rib cage. Scapula. Clavicle. Pelvis. Humerus. Radius. Ulna. Hands. Femur. Tibia. Fibula. Feet. Do not accidentally invent random bones. Do not create impossible joints unless distortion is intentionally part of the artwork. Artistic transformation is welcome. Anatomical mistakes are not artistic transformation. USE THE HUMAN BODY. Bone is uniquely suited to body-aware tattoo composition. A skeletal hand can interact with the real hand. Vertebrae can follow the spine. Ribs can interact with the torso. A shoulder joint can relate to the actual shoulder. A jaw can follow anatomical curves. A skeleton can climb an arm. A skull can rotate around a shoulder. A skeleton can curl around a thigh. Do not simply place anatomical diagrams onto skin. THE BODY CAN BECOME PART OF THE ANATOMICAL IDEA. USE THE FULL WORLD OF TATTOOING. Black and Grey Realism. Fine Line. Dotwork. Blackwork. Sketch. Engraving. Woodcut. Illustrative. Neo-Traditional. American Traditional. New School. Geometric. Negative Space. Watercolour. Colour Realism. Surrealism. Ornamental. And intelligent combinations. Do not mechanically rotate styles. Choose the artistic language that makes the idea stronger. MEDIEVAL AND ENGRAVING WORK SHOULD FEEL AUTHENTICALLY DRAWN. Use: Cross-hatching. Parallel engraving lines. Woodcut texture. Dense directional shading. Old anatomical illustration. Memento mori. Gothic architecture. Medieval figures. Historical visual language. Do not merely apply a vintage filter to modern clip art. BLACKWORK SHOULD USE BLACK AS STRUCTURE. Allow bone to emerge from darkness. Use natural skin as bone. Use negative space. Allow sections of anatomy to disappear. Use large black fields when appropriate. Use brush gestures. Use silhouette. Do not simply shade every skull dark grey. FINE LINE SHOULD REMAIN ARTISTICALLY AMBITIOUS. Fine Line skulls do not need flowers. Explore unusual anatomical angles. Skeletal hands. Spines. Pelvis. Movement. Full skeletons. Relationships between figures. Scientific drawing. Construction. COLOR IS WELCOME. Bone does not need to be painted random colors. Color can come from light. Neon. Water. Fire. Stained glass. Sunset. Reflections. Atmosphere. Environmental light. Ivory bone can receive: Cobalt light. Crimson light. Emerald light. Violet shadow. Turquoise underwater reflection. Warm gold. Color should make physical or artistic sense. Do not turn every colorful skull into a sugar skull. DIA DE LOS MUERTOS AND CALAVERA IMAGERY HAVE SPECIFIC CULTURAL CONTEXT. Treat those traditions accurately and respectfully when intentionally used. Do not use Mexican cultural imagery merely as generic colorful skull decoration. ANIMAL SKELETONS BELONG. Explore: Bird skeletons. Snake skeletons. Bat skeletons. Horse skeletons. Whale skeletons. Ram skulls. Deer skulls. Crocodile skulls. Shark jaws. Prehistoric skeletons. Other fascinating skeletal anatomy. Do not simply repeat the Animals category with bones. The skeletal structure itself should be the artistic reason for the design. SURREALISM CAN GO FAR. A skull can contain architecture. An eye socket can become a doorway. A rib cage can contain an ocean. A skeleton can become roots. A skull can contain a storm. Bone can become birds. Bone can become architecture. Bone can become geometry. A skeleton can escape another skeleton. A skull can separate into layers. Scale can become impossible. But maintain visual hierarchy. Do not create random surreal collage. THE SKULL OR SKELETON MUST REMAIN THE HERO. DARKNESS IS WELCOME. Do not make everything dark. Do not make everything beautiful. Do not make everything aggressive. Do not make everything solemn. Bone can be: Quiet. Romantic. Scientific. Sexual. Tender. Brutal. Elegant. Terrifying. Peaceful. Funny when intentionally appropriate. Ancient. Futuristic. Minimal. Monumental. THE CLICHES ARE ALLOWED. THE CLICHES ARE NOT ALLOWED TO CONTROL THE CATEGORY. Skull and rose. Skull and snake. Skull and crown. Skull and dagger. Skull and moth. Skull and clock. These are established tattoo subjects. Use them when the design is genuinely exceptional. Do not produce endless variations because they are easy. Do not surround every skull with flowers. Do not put a crown on every skull. Do not put a snake through every eye socket. Do not make every skeleton a grim reaper. Do not make every skeleton comedic. Do not make every skull realistic. Do not make every design Black and Grey. Do not make every skull face forward. Do not make every skeleton stand upright. Do not make everything about death. BONE IS THE MATERIAL. THE ART CAN GO ANYWHERE. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
